              "rawText": "FILE: GI-Drug used in Dyspepsia, GERD and PUD.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 6 GI\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UVqepEPTX_lfDVTGX--2iyuivqRzjlU9/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 31\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nDrug used in Dyspepsia \nGERD and PUD\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nAntacid\n2\n▪ Antacid pH > 4 → to neutralize acid, inactivate pepsin, bind \nbile salts, enhance mucosal defense\n▪ Effect: symptom relief and ulcer healing (poor)\n▪ No longer used as monotherapy → low efficacy, \ninconvenience, ADRs\n▪ Take pc. is better than ac. >> longer neutralize 3-4 hours\nFeature NaHCO3 CaCO3 MgOH2 AlOH3\nOnset Rapid Intermediate Rapid Slow\nDuration Short Moderate Moderate Moderate \nAlkalosis Yes ? No No \nStool --- Constipation Diarrhea Constipation\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nAntacid\n3\n▪ Antacids with high acid neutralizing capacity (ANC) are more \neffective\n▪ Sodium bicarbonate, Calcium carbonate → greatest \nANC BUT unacceptable SE → alkalosis \n▪ Most widely used: Magnesium and Aluminum hydroxide \n▪ Sodium bicarbonate is readily absorbed while CaCO 3, \nAluminum hydroxide and Magnesium hydroxide are poorly \nabsorbed.\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nAntacid\n4\n▪ Dosage form\n▪ Tablet\n▪ Mg(OH)2 200-400 mg + Al(OH)3 200-600 mg\n▪ +/- Kaolin 50 mg or Simeticone 40-125 mg\n▪ Regimen 1-2 tab tid-qid pc\n▪ Suspension\n▪ Mg(OH)2 103-500 mg/tsp + Al(OH)3 165-650 mg/tsp\n▪ +/- Simeticone 20-40 mg/tsp\n▪ Regimen 30 ml tid-qid pc\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nAntacid\n5\n▪ Dosage form\n▪ Tablet, Suspension (per 10 ml)\n▪ NaHCO3 95-300 mg\n▪ CaCO3 70-360 mg\n▪ Sodium alginate 250-500 mg\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nAntacid\n6\n▪ ADRs\n▪ GI side effect:  \n▪ Magnesium → diarrhea\n▪ Aluminum and calcium → constipation\n▪ Aluminum and magnesium accumulation in patient with \nrenal disease\n▪ Systemic alkalosis → Sodium bicarbonate\n▪ Rebound hyperacidity and milk alkali syndrome → \nCaCO3\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nAntacid\n7\n▪ Drug interaction\n▪ Calcium, Aluminum and Magnesium : decrease \nabsorption of tetracycline, fluoroquinolone, iron, \nwarfarin, digoxin, phenytoin, ketoconazole\n▪ Aluminum forms insoluble salts with phosphorus dietary\n▪ Take antacid separately → at least 2 hours \n▪ Warning\n▪ Sodium content: patients with CHF\n▪ Magnesium and aluminum content: patients with renal \nimpairment (ClCr < 30 ml/min)\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n8\n▪ Cimetidine, Ranitidine, Famotidine\n▪ MOA : competitively and reversibly bind to H2 receptors on \nparietal cells\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n9\n▪ ADRs\n▪ Most common: GI upset, diarrhea, drowsiness, \nheadache\n▪ Other: dizziness, confusion, (elderly: cimetidine → rash \nand sweating)\n▪ Gynecomastia: impotence can occur in men with \nprolonged or high dose of “cimetidine” ( antiandrogenic\neffect)\n▪ Thrombocytopenia → reversible\n▪ Ranitidine → less side effect \n▪ Famotidine, nizatidine → NO antiandrogenic effect\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n10\n▪ Drug interaction\n▪ Cimetidine: interact with drugs metabolized by CYP 3A4 \nEx. Theophylline, phenytoin, warfarin, beta -blockers, \nlidocaine, diazepam\n▪ Ranitidine: Less interact with hepatic CYP system, \ndecrease renal clearance of procainamide, increase \neffect of glipizide.\n▪ Famotidine and nizatidine: less affect to hepatic \nclearance and other drugs\n▪ Antacid, metoclopamide: decrease bioavailability of \ncimetidine separate admin time at least 2 hours\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n11\nBasic heterocyclic \ngroup\nFlexible chain\n(4 atom is optimal)\nPolar group\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n12\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nH2 receptor antagonists\n13\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n14\n▪ Omeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole, \nesomeprazole\n▪ MOA : irreversibly bind to H+/K+ ATPase in parietal cell\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n15\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n16\n▪ NOT suitable for long term maintenance due to \n“achlorhydria” (no acid in stomach) or rebound hyperacidity\n▪ ADR\n▪ Most common SE: diarrhea, constipation, nausea, \nabdominal colic, headache\n▪ Long term used → facture risk, anemia (B12 \ndeficiency)\n▪ Omeprazole: long term in rats → hyperplasia of \nenterochromaffin like cell, gastric carcinoid tumor BUT \nno evidence in human\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n17\n▪ Drug interaction\n▪ Omeprazole : CYP2C9, CYP2C19 inhibitor decrease \nelimination of warfarin, phenytoin, diazepam, \ncyclosporin\n▪ All PPIs decrease absorption of ketoconazole and \nitraconazole that require an acidic environment for \nabsorption\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n18\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n19\nPPIs Acid\nAcid labile\nDrug degradation\nEnteric coated\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n20\n▪ Drug formulation\n▪ Delayed-release capsule/multiple unit pellets system\n▪ Omeprazole (miracid®, losec MUPS®)\n▪ Lansoprazole (prevacid FDT®)\n▪ Esomeprazole (nexium MUPs®)\n▪ Delayed-release tablet or enteric-coated tablet\n▪ Pantoprazole (controloc®)\n▪ Rabeprazole (pariet®)\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n21\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nProton pump inhibitors\n22\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nP-CAB\n23\n▪ Inhibits gastric acid secretion by reversibly binding to and \nblocking the gastric proton pump (H +/K+-ATPase) in \nstomach parietal cells.\nPotassium-competitive acid blocker (P-CAB)\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nP-CAB\n24\n▪ The most common side effects are constipation, diarrhea, \nenlarged feeling of abdomen, nausea, rash, and edema.\n▪ Long-term use may lead to vitamin B12 deficiency, \nhypomagnesemia, and fundic gland polyps.\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nSucrafate\n25\n▪ Exposed to gastric acid → form viscous adhesive → bind to \nprotein molecule in the ulcer.\n▪ Stimulate bicarbonate and mucus secretion.\n▪ ADRs : constipation, indigestion, dry mouth, dizziness, \nhypophosphatemia\n▪ Drug interaction : ↓ BA & Effect warfarin, ketoclonazole, \nphenytoin, digoxin, theophylline, cimetidine\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nBismuth subsalicylate\n26\n▪ Local gastroprotective effect\n▪ ADRs : Prolong use (>8 weeks) → neurotoxicity, renal \ninsufficiency\n▪ DI : food, milk and antacid\n▪ Drug of choice in pregnancy\n▪ *** Black stool from bismuth sulfide***\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\nMisoprostol\n27\n▪ Derivative of prostaglandin E1 (PGE1)\n▪ Prevention of NSAIDs-induced GU\n▪ ADRs: \n▪ Diarrhea (up to 38%), minimized by taking drug with or \nafter meals\n▪ Abdominal pain, menstrual irregularity\n▪ ***Abortion *** induce uterine contraction\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nAntiflatulence\n28\n▪ Simethicone\n▪ Often combines with antacid\n▪ Combination of dimethylpolysiloxane and siliga gel\n▪ Antifoaming agent: reduce surface tension of gas \nbubble >> relief gaseous distention\n▪ Others: M. Carminative (capsicum ,cardamon and ginger), \nNa bicarbonate (sodamint)\n▪ Sodamint: limit used as antacid → systemic alkalosis\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\nDopamine antagonist\n29\nMetoclopamide\n▪ Dopamine antagonist (CNS & GI)\n▪ Promote GI motility\n▪ Indirect acting cholinergic effect\n▪ ↑GI movement → Acetylcholine\n▪ Raise esophageal sphincter pressure\n▪ Antiemetic effect\n▪ ADRs :\n▪ Dry mouth, nervousness, drowsiness, dystonic reaction, \nblurred vision ***extrapyramidal symptoms***\n\n--- [Page 30] ---\nDopamine antagonist\n30\nDomperidone\n▪ Blocks D2 receptor at CTZ, outside BBB → antiemetic effect, \nhasten gastric emptying time\n▪ No effect on releasing of Acetylcholine\n▪ ADRs : Abdominal cramp, increase prolactin level\n▪ EPS → high dose and children\n▪ DI : anticholinergic drug\n▪ QT prolongation\nNot more than 30 mg/day\nNot recommend in young patient (<10 years)\n\n--- [Page 31] ---\n31"
