          "driveFiles": [
            {
              "id": "1-WEVqmP29Ygf2tsnsoyaErz95H9aOqIW",
              "name": "Gynecology-Dysmenorrhea.pdf",
              "folder": "Gynecology",
              "rawText": "=== PAGE 1 ===\nDYSMENORRHEA\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\n￼\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nDefinition\nDysmenorrhea is one of the most commonly encountered\ngynecologic complaints. It is defined as crampy pelvic\npain occurring with or just prior to menses.\n➢ Primary dysmenorrhea*** implies pain in the setting of normal\npelvic anatomy and physiology.\n➢ Secondary dysmenorrhea is associated with underlying pelvic\npathology.\n➢ Endometriosis\n➢ Leiomyoma uteri\n➢ Adenomyosis\n➢ Ovarian cyst\n➢ Pelvic congestion\n2\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nPathophysiology3\n￼\nRef: http://clinicalgate.com/pelvic-pain-2/\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nRisk factors\n■ Young age\n■ Heavy menses\n■ Nulliparity\n■ Early menarche\n■ Cigarette smoking\n5\nRef: Pharmacotherapy 9th edition\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nSymptoms\n■ About 90% experience symptoms within 2 years of\nmenarche.\n■ Patients complain of crampy pelvic pain beginning\nshortly before or at the onset of menses.\n■ Symptoms typically last from 1 to 3 days\n■ Associated Symptoms\n– Nausea and vomiting\n– Fatigue\n– Diarrhea\n– Lower backache\n– Headache\n6\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nTreatment7\n￼\nTreatment algorithm for dysmenorrhea. (IUD, intrauterine device; MPA, medroxyprogesterone\nacetate; NSAIDs, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs; OC, oral contraceptive\nRef: Pharmacotherapy 9th edition\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\n￼\nNon-pharmacotherapy\n■ Topical heat therapy\n■ Exercise\n■ Low-fat vegetarian diet\n■ Transcutaneous electric nerve stimulation (TENS)\n■ Acupressure\n■ Acupuncture\n8\n￼\n\n=== PAGE 8 ===\nPharmacotherapy\n■ NSAIDs\n■ Oral contraceptives\n■ Medroxyprogesterone acetate\n■ Intrauterine device\n9\n\n=== PAGE 9 ===\nNSAIDs\n■ NSAIDs are the initial treatment of choice.\n■ These agents do not differ in efficacy.\n■ The most commonly used agents are naproxen and\nibuprofen.\n■ All NSAIDs have a propensity for causing GI distress\nand ulceration; their administration with food or milk\nminimizes these effects.\n■ In women who have a history of NSAID-induced gastric\neffects, the use of celecoxib, a cyclo-oxygenase-2\n(COX-2) inhibitor, is an alternative.\n10\n\n=== PAGE 10 ===\nNSAIDs\n■ If an NSAID or celecoxib use is contraindicated or not\ndesired, hormonal agents should be considered.\n■ Acetaminophen is inferior to NSAID in treatment of this\ndisorder.\n11\n￼\nSOGC clinical practice guideline 2005.\n\n=== PAGE 11 ===\nOral contraceptives\n■ OCs improve dysmenorrhea by inhibiting endometrial\ntissue proliferation which reduces endometrial-derived\nprostaglandins that cause the pelvic pain.\n■ Relieve symptom within 3-6 month.\n■ Significant improvements in mild, moderate, and severe\ndysmenorrhea.\n12\n\n=== PAGE 12 ===\nDepot MPA\n■ Can be considered for dysmenorrhea treatment.\n■ Its efficacy is secondary to its ability to render most\npatients amenorrheic within 6 to 12 months of use.\n■ Because the pelvic pain of dysmenorrhea is related to\nthe prostaglandins released during menses, in the\nsetting of amenorrhea the underlying cause of\ndysmenorrhea is removed.\n13\n\n=== PAGE 13 ===\nSummary14\nRef: Pharmacotherapy 9th edition\n￼\n\n=== PAGE 14 ===\nSummary15\nRef: Pharmacotherapy 9th edition\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n\n=== PAGE 15 ===\nSummary16\nRef: Pharmacotherapy 9th edition\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n￼\n\n=== PAGE 16 (OCR) ===\nthauk\nyou.\n\n\n"
            }
          ]
