              "totalPages": 45,
              "rawText": "FILE: Oncology-Breast cancer.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 11 Oncology\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Nk3wLW2Oxt_BrQ-V-uksvDbB5t6uDPli/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 45\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN, PHARM.D.\nPHARMA PLUS INSTITUTE\nBREAST CANCER\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nEtiology\n2\n■ Hereditary breast cancer\n– BRCA \n– P53: Tumor suppressor gene\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nEtiology\n3\n■ Progression genes\n– HER2 (Human Epidermal Growth Factor \nReceptor-2, ErbB-2) proto-oncogene\n30% of women with breast cancer have HER2 positive breast cancer\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nEtiology\n4\n■ Progression genes\n– HER2 testing\n■ Immunohistochemistry(IHC)\n■ Fluorescence In-situ hybridization (FISH)**\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nEtiology\n5\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nEtiology\n6\n■ Tumorigenesis\nDuctal carcinoma in situ (DCIS)\nLobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS)\nInvasive ductal carcinoma (IDC)\nhttps://tel.archives-ouvertes.fr/tel-00814265\nTGF-α\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nRisk factors\n7\n■ Age: >50% risk occurs after age of 60 years\n■ Family history of breast cancer\n■ Endogenous estrogen exposure\n■ Exogenous estrogen exposure\n■ Benign breast disease \n■ Radiation exposure\n■ Obesity\n■ Alcohol\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nScreening\n8\n■ Clinical Breast Examination (CBE)\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nScreening\n9\n■ Breast self-examination (BSE)\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nScreening\n10\n■ Mammography\nOBG Manag. 2017 November;29(11):24, 26, 28\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nScreening\n11\n■ Mammography\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\n12\n\n--- [Page 13] ---\nClinical presentation\n13\n\n--- [Page 14] ---\nDiagnosis\n15\n■ History and Physical Exam\n■ Biopsy\n– FNA (4%-10% false negative rate)\n– Core-needle biopsy (3% false negative rate)\n– Excisional biopsy\n■ Genetic\n– PR, ER, HER-2\nFine needle aspiration: FNA\nProgestin receptor: PR\nEstrogen receptor: ER\n\n--- [Page 15] ---\nDiagnosis\n16\nArticle contributed by SingHealth Duke-NUS Breast Centre\n\n--- [Page 16] ---\nDiagnosis\n17\nArticle contributed by SingHealth Duke-NUS Breast Centre\n\n--- [Page 17] ---\nStaging\n\n--- [Page 18] ---\nStaging\n\n--- [Page 19] ---\nSubtype\nN Engl J Med 2009;360:790-800.\n\n--- [Page 20] ---\nSubtype\n\n--- [Page 21] ---\nSubtype\nN Engl J Med 2009;360:790-800.\n\n--- [Page 22] ---\nPrognostic factors\n■ Age at diagnosis\n■ Race\n■ Alcohol use, exercise, diet\n■ Disease characteristics (Tumor size, LN status, \nhistological subtypes, nuclear or histological \ngrade, proliferation indices) \n■ Hormone receptor status (ER/PR)\n■ HER-2 gene status\n\n--- [Page 23] ---\nPrognostic\nRupninder Sandhu, Joel S. Parker, Wendell D. Jones, et al. LABMEDICINE.2010;41:364-72.\n\n--- [Page 24] ---\nPrognostic\nImage source: BreastCancer.Org / breastcancer.org/symptoms/diagnosis/prognosis\n\n--- [Page 25] ---\nPrinciple of treatment\n26\nINTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF ONCOLOGY 47: 437-445, 2015\nER=estrogen receptor\nPR=progestin receptor\n\n--- [Page 26] ---\nTreatment\n27\n■ Non-invasive breast cancer\n– Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS)\n– Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) \n■ Invasive breast cancer\n\n--- [Page 27] ---\n28\n\n--- [Page 28] ---\nSummary: LCIS\n29\n■ Observation (low risk of invasive breast cancer \n21% over 15 years)\n■ Surveillance\n■ Bilateral mastectomy ± reconstruction\n■ Considered endocrine therapy\n– Tamoxifen\n– Aromatase inhibitors\n\n--- [Page 29] ---\n30\n\n--- [Page 31] ---\nSummary: DCIS\n32\n■ Lumpectomy\n■ Mastectomy \n■ Reconstruction\n■ Sentinel node biopsy\n■ Whole breast radiation\n■ Considered endocrine therapy\n– Tamoxifen\n– Aromatase inhibitors\n\n--- [Page 32] ---\n33\nMastectomy\nReconstruction\n\n--- [Page 33] ---\n34\nAxillary lymph nodes dissection (ALND)\n\n--- [Page 38] ---\nSummary: Invasive \n39\n■ Early invasive breast cancer\n– Locoregional treatment\n■ Lumpectomy\n■ Mastectomy \n■ Reconstruction\n– Radiation\n– Chemotherapy (neoadjuvant)\n– Endocrine therapy\n– Trastuzumab (If HER-2 positive)\n\n--- [Page 39] ---\nSummary: Invasive \n40\n■ Advanced invasive breast cancer\n– Chemotherapy (adjuvant)\n– Endocrine therapy\n– Trastuzumab (If HER-2 positive)\n– Radiation\n– Locoregional treatment\n■ Lumpectomy\n■ Mastectomy \n■ Reconstruction\n\n--- [Page 40] ---\nTamoxifen\n41\n■ Estrogen receptor antagonist at breast tissue\n■ ADR:\n– Thromboembolic events\n– Uterine bleeding\n– Endometrial cancer\n– Vasomotor complication\n■ Metabolism via CYP2D6\n\n--- [Page 41] ---\nAromatase inhibitors\n42\n■ Anastrozole 1 mg OD\n■ Letrozole 2.5 mg OD\n■ Exemestane 25 mg OD\n■ Side effects: diaphoresis, hot flushes, night \nsweats, bone pain, bank pain, fatigue, \nhypertension\n\n--- [Page 42] ---\n43\nN Engl J Med 2003;348:2431-42.\nBreast Cancer July 2018, Volume 25, Issue 4, pp 379–391\n\n--- [Page 43] ---\nTrastuzumab\n44\n■ Anti-HER-2 receptor\n■ ADR:\n– Cardiotoxicity\n– Pain\n– Fever, chills, cough\n– Nausea, vomiting\n\n--- [Page 44] ---\n45\n\n--- [Page 45] ---\n46"
