          "url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oO0LPyCLP-ccRDMSwu602IhIO0aw5hqK/view",
          "totalPages": 12,
          "rawText": "FILE: 2026 Pulmonary-Drug induce pulmonary disorders.pdf\nFOLDER: Chapter 13 Pulmonary\nDRIVE_URL: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oO0LPyCLP-ccRDMSwu602IhIO0aw5hqK/view\nTOTAL_PAGES: 12\n\n--- [Page 1] ---\nPONGSIRI KULCHANAWICHIEN\nPHARM.D., DIP CLIN EPI.\nFELLOW OF THE COLLEGE OF COMMUNITY PHARMACY (FCCP)\nCERTIFICATE OF PHARMACY (PHARMACOGENOMIC AND PRECISION MEDICINE)\nDRUG INDUCED \nPULMONARY DISORDERS\n\n--- [Page 2] ---\nDrug induced cough\n■ ACEIs \n■ All the ACE inhibitors can induce a dry, \npersistent, and often nocturnal cough.\n■ The cough can begin within 3 days or have a \ndelayed onset of up to 12 months following \ninitiation of ACE inhibitor therapy.\n■ The cough remits within 1 to 4 days of \ndiscontinuing therapy but (rarely) can last up to \n4 weeks and recur with rechallenge.\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n2\n\n--- [Page 3] ---\nDrug induced cough\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n3\n\n--- [Page 4] ---\nDrug-induced bronchospasm\n■ Aspirin and NSAIDs\n– Bronchospasm typically begins within minutes \nto hours following ingestion of aspirin and is \nassociated with rhinorrhea, flushing of the \nhead and neck, and conjunctivitis.\n■ Beta-blockers\n– β-Adrenergic receptor blockers comprise the \nother large class of drugs that can be \nhazardous to a person with asthma. \n– Even the more cardioselective agents such as \nacebutolol, atenolol, and metoprolol have been \nreported to cause asthma attacks.\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n4\n\n--- [Page 5] ---\nDrug-induced bronchospasm\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n5\n\n--- [Page 6] ---\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n6\n\n--- [Page 7] ---\nDrug-induced bronchospasm\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n7\nSmooth muscle contraction\n(Bronchoconstriction)\n\n--- [Page 8] ---\nDrug-induced bronchospasm\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n8\n\n--- [Page 9] ---\nDrug-induced \nInterstitial pneumonitis\n■ Amiodarone\n– Interstitial pneumonitis is the most \ncommon presentation of amiodarone-\ninduced pulmonary disease.\n– Interstitial pneumonitis usually presents \nafter two or more months of therapy, \nespecially in patients in whom the dose of \namiodarone exceeds 400 mg per day.\n– Treatment: discontinuation of amiodarone\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n9\n\n--- [Page 10] ---\nDrug-induced \nInterstitial fibrosis\n■ Bleomycin\n– The major limitation of bleomycin therapy \nis the potential for life -threatening \ninterstitial pulmonary fibrosis in up to 10 % \nof patients receiving the drug.\n– Treatment: discontinuation of bleomycin\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n10\n\n--- [Page 11] ---\nDrug-induced \nPulmonary edema\n■ Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema\n– Heroin\n■ Cardiogenic pulmonary edema\n– Beta-blockers\n– Excessive IV fluid\nCOPYRIGHT © PHARMA PLUS  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED\n11\n\n--- [Page 12] ---\n12"
